# The two authorities this cluster signs with, and they are two because the two
# certificates answer two different questions.
#
# Generated by deploy/terraform/gcp. Edit the template, not this file.
---
# What the node itself presents on the gRPC listener, and what it dials a peer
# with. Self-signed, because two operators in a federation share no authority to
# appeal to and what identifies a peer is the public key it published in its own
# discovery document (C12). A chain nobody checks is a cost with no buyer.
apiVersion: cert-manager.io/v1
kind: ClusterIssuer
metadata:
  name: quire-selfsigned
spec:
  selfSigned: {}
---
# What the gateway presents for the discovery documents, which a peer verifies
# the ordinary way against an ordinary trust store.
#
# The challenge is DNS-01 and not HTTP-01, and that is C23 arriving in a third
# place. This domain resolves to a load balancer that passes TCP through to a
# gateway holding no certificate yet — so there is nothing on the HTTP path for
# an HTTP-01 challenge to be answered by, and the answer has to be a record in
# the zone instead. There is no serviceAccountSecretRef: cert-manager
# authenticates as the Google service account its Kubernetes one is bound to,
# and there is no key anywhere in this deployment.
apiVersion: cert-manager.io/v1
kind: ClusterIssuer
metadata:
  name: quire-public
spec:
  acme:
    server: ${acme_server}
    email: ${acme_email}
    privateKeySecretRef:
      name: quire-acme-account
    solvers:
      - dns01:
          cloudDNS:
            project: ${project}
